tty and The Heartbreakers","year":2010,"weight":3,"yt":"3_iBKacXIA4","src":"musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":115932688871073,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Heartland Rock","rank":83,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":1}},{"id":154148339192746,"type":"album","name":"Hypnotic Eye","artist":"Tom Petty and The Heartbreakers","year":2014,"weight":3,"yt":"TGyOz9H5FmI","src":"musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":115932688871073,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Heartland Rock","rank":51,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":1}},{"id":108806136731613,"type":"album","name":"Standing in the Way of Shadows (Compilation Box Set)","artist":"Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers","year":null,"weight":4,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[{"name":"Music Radar Clan","n":1}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}}],"members":[{"id":41944211054257,"type":"artist","name":"Tom Petty","artist":null,"year":1950,"weight":134,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":244763212941215,"type":"artist","name":"The Heartbreakers","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":45,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"member_of":[],"genres":[{"id":220327530487024,"type":"genre","name":"New Wave","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1514,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":106381401668595,"type":"genre","name":"Heartland Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":254,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":106381401668595,"type":"genre","name":"Heartland Rock","n":13},{"id":186201380116213,"type":"genre","name":"classic rock","n":2},{"id":121661734395550,"type":"genre","name":"Power Pop","n":2},{"id":109063683126539,"type":"genre","name":"Hard Rock","n":2},{"id":18872195504512,"type":"genre","name":"Pop Rock","n":2},{"id":211270792239401,"type":"genre","name":"Roots Rock","n":1},{"id":210192360016800,"type":"genre","name":"Alternative Rock","n":1},{"id":151685778897301,"type":"genre","name":"Southern Rock","n":1},{"id":53681850040905,"type":"genre","name":"Rock & Roll","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Breakthrough with Even the Losers","body":"The release of \"Even the Losers\" as a single in the summer of 1980 is considered the breakthrough moment for Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, establishing their presence and impact in the music scene. \"I think it's fair to say it was the breakthrough for Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"California Band Identity","body":"Despite Tom Petty's southern roots, he made it clear that he considered the Heartbreakers to be a \"California band,\" aligning them with the tradition of other influential California groups like Buffalo Springfield, The Flying Burrito Brothers, and The Beach Boys. This perspective influenced their musical identity and where Petty chose to reside for most of his life. \"he made it clear that he considered the heartbreakers to be a california band in the tradition of buffalo springfield the flying burrito brothers and even the beach boys and that's why.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Final Performance and Message with American Girl","body":"On the final tour of Tom Petty's life, the song \"American Girl\" served as the closing number, which also amounted to being the last song he ever performed publicly. During this performance, a video screen showcased a montage of diverse women, including white, African American, Latina in military uniform, older, middle-aged, and transgender women, symbolizing \"actual American women.\" This poignant act, delivered just before his death, communicated a powerful message about evolution and broadening perspectives, making it potentially his most significant final public gesture to fans. \"This is the last thing that Petty did for his fans before his death And maybe the most significant thing he did Is this idea of evolution and how we can think And maybe even broaden how we think.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 55 - Heartland Rock is about More than the Heartland","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Handclaps for Linn LM-1 Samples","body":"The handclap samples included in the Linn LM-1 drum computer were provided by the members of Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers. \"handclaps. They came courtesy of the guys in Tom Petty and the heartbreakers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of the Drum Machine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dablAKDOOV0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Influence from Gram Parsons","body":"Tom Petty & The Heartbreakers are included among the \"descendants\" influenced by The Flying Burrito Brothers' seminal album \"Gilded Palace of Sin,\" illustrating Gram Parsons' enduring legacy on subsequent rock acts. \"And you can even throw Tom Petty the heartbreakers in there.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-35-cosmic-american-music-70s-country-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Musical Style","body":"The band specialized in short, sharp, choppy songs that heavily featured rhythm guitar and conveyed a vaguely anti-corporate message. This style aligned them with the burgeoning New Wave movement. \"Tom Petty specialized in short, sharp choppy songs with lots of rhythm guitar and vaguely anti-corporate stents.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 6","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iL9KpQFqH4o"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"New Wave Branding","body":"Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, a relatively new band from Florida, were categorized as New Wave. This classification was based on their shared musical characteristics with other New Wave acts like Talking Heads, Blondie, and Elvis Costello. \"And so as this new band from Florida called Tom Petty in the Heartbreakers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 6","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iL9KpQFqH4o"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Sonic Similarities with Red Hot Chili Peppers","body":"Tom Petty's \"Mary Jane's Last Dance\" (1993) was noted for sounding very similar to the Red Hot Chili Peppers' \"Dani California\" (2006). Although people suggested Petty considered it, he never sued the Red Hot Chili Peppers over the resemblance. \"Petty never sued but people sure did suggest that he thought about it.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Sonic Coincidences in Alt-Rock Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L09R2evjyMc"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2002)","body":"Won.","year":2002,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q179191"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"star on Hollywood Walk of Fame","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17985761"}]}],"concerts":[{"kind":"concert","title":"Farm Aid","body":null,"year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[]}],"xref":[{"kind":"xref","title":"Fan and Album Support","body":"Stan Lynch, the drummer for Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, was a fan and provided assistance in recording her 1994 album, *Stop Messin with My Mind*. \"She had a fan in Stan Lynch, the drummer for Tom Petty in the Heartbreakers, and he helped her record an album in 1994 called Stop Messin with My Mind.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"The Amazing Dolores","from_id":66512336948561,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Still More Outsider Music","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HcK4M7-02ik"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Musical Style","body":"Elvis Costello specialized in short, sharp, choppy songs, featuring a significant amount of rhythm guitar and a vaguely anti-corporate stance. This musical approach was similar to that of Talking Heads, Blondie, and Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, all of whom were categorized under the New Wave banner. \"like the talking heads and blondie and Elvis Costello, Tom Petty specialized in short, sharp choppy songs with lots of rhythm guitar and vaguely anti-corporate stents.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Elvis Costello","from_id":91466902181829,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 6","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iL9KpQFqH4o"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"New Wave Artist","body":"Elvis Costello was identified as a New Wave artist, grouped alongside bands such as Talking Heads, Blondie, and Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, indicating his contribution to the evolving sound of modern rock. \"The talking heads and blondie and Elvis Costello were new wave.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Elvis Costello","from_id":91466902181829,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 6","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iL9KpQFqH4o"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Production/Engineering Credits","body":"Sylvia Massy worked behind the board for a wide array of prominent artists, including Aerosmith, Prince, Bobby Brown, the Red Hot Chili Peppers, Slayer, Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers, Katting Kage, Johnny Cash, a Taylor Hawkins and Civil Project, System of a Down, The Smashing Pumpkins, The Black Crowes, and The Beastie Boys. This extensive list showcases her versatility and expertise across genres. \"Since then, she's worked behind the board for Erasmith, Prince, Bobby Brown, the Red Hot Chili Peppers Slayer, Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers Catering Cache, Johnny Cash, a Taylor Hawkins and Civil Project, System of a Down, The Smashing Pumpkins, The Black Cros and The Beastie Boys.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Sylvia Massy","from_id":46251030672109,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Driven By Her: Unsung Heroines","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oRR7N-YUcRE"}]}],"charts":[{"id":71853013123217,"type":"album","name":"Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers","work_year":1976,"list_id":192934750972716,"list":"1001 Albums You Must Hear Before You Die","src":"charts","rank":347,"year":1976,"note":null,"yt":"Phyn7zacbRc","scaruffi":null},{"id":71853013123217,"type":"album","name":"Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers","list_id":115932688871073,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Heartland Rock","src":"musthear","rank":39,"year":1976,"note":null,"yt":"Phyn7zacbRc","scaruffi":null},{"id":71853013123217,"type":"album","name":"Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers","work_year":1976,"list_id":65232636722211,"list":"Scaruffi 1970s","src":"charts","rank":144,"year":1976,"note":null,"yt":"Phyn7zacbRc","scaruffi":null},{"id":207044719394447,"type":"album","name":"You're Gonna Get It!","list_id":115932688871073,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Heartland Rock","src":"musthear","rank":79,"year":1978,"note":null,"yt":"xc1